exercises.reference:
def classify(pH):
    if pH < 7:
        return "acidic"
    elif pH == 7:
        return "neutral"
    else:
        return "alkaline"

print(classify(2.0))
print(classify(7.0))
print(classify(8.3))

exercises.solution:
Each branch returns one of the three words:

```python
def classify(pH):
    if pH < 7:
        return "acidic"
    elif pH == 7:
        return "neutral"
    else:
        return "alkaline"
```

Python checks the conditions from top to bottom: below 7 gives `"acidic"`; otherwise, exactly 7 gives `"neutral"`; anything left must be above 7, so `"alkaline"`.

Two honest caveats a chemist would add: a measured pH is never *exactly* 7.000…, so real instruments treat values close to 7 as neutral; and neutral is pH 7 only at 25 °C (at 37 °C it is about 6.8).
